## The Eigenvalue Method

Consider the homogeneous system with a constant matrix $\A$:
$$\X'=\A\X.$$ {#eq:hom}

- Try a solution of the form $\X=\K e^{\lambda t}$, where $\lambda$ is a scalar and $\K\neq\mathbf 0$ is a constant vector, both to be determined. Then $\X'=\lambda e^{\lambda t}\K$.
- Substituting into (@eq:hom): $\lambda e^{\lambda t}\K=e^{\lambda t}\A\K$, i.e. $e^{\lambda t}(\A-\lambda I)\K=\mathbf 0$, so
  $$(\A-\lambda I)\K=\mathbf 0.$$
- Since $\K\neq\mathbf 0$, we need $|\A-\lambda I|=0$.

::: keybox
$\X=\K e^{\lambda t}$ solves $\X'=\A\X$ !!if and only if!! $\lambda$ is an eigenvalue of $\A$ and $\K$ is a corresponding eigenvector.
:::

The eigenvalues fall into three cases: **distinct real**, **repeated real**, and **complex**.

### Distinct real eigenvalues

::: theorem "Distinct real eigenvalues" {#thm:distinct}
If the $n\times n$ matrix $\A$ has $n$ distinct real eigenvalues $\lambda_1,\dots,\lambda_n$ with eigenvectors $\K_1,\dots,\K_n$, then $\K_1,\dots,\K_n$ are linearly independent,
$$\X_1=\K_1e^{\lambda_1t},\quad \X_2=\K_2e^{\lambda_2t},\quad\dots,\quad \X_n=\K_ne^{\lambda_nt}$$
is a fundamental set of (@eq:hom) on $(-\infty,\infty)$, and the general solution is
$$\boxed{\X=c_1\K_1e^{\lambda_1t}+c_2\K_2e^{\lambda_2t}+\cdots+c_n\K_ne^{\lambda_nt}}$$
:::

::: example {#ex:saddle}
Find a general solution of $\dfrac{dx}{dt}=2x+3y,\quad \dfrac{dy}{dt}=2x+y.$
:::

::: solution steps space=10
Here $\A=\begin{pmatrix}2&3\\2&1\end{pmatrix}$ and $|\A-\lambda I|=(2-\lambda)(1-\lambda)-6=\lambda^2-3\lambda-4=(\lambda-4)(\lambda+1)=0$, so $\lambda_1=4,\ \lambda_2=-1$.
>>>
$\lambda_1=4$: $\begin{pmatrix}-2&3\\2&-3\end{pmatrix}\K=\mathbf 0\Rightarrow 2k_1=3k_2,\quad \K_1=\begin{pmatrix}3\\2\end{pmatrix}.$

$\lambda_2=-1$: $\begin{pmatrix}3&3\\2&2\end{pmatrix}\K=\mathbf 0\Rightarrow k_1=-k_2,\quad \K_2=\begin{pmatrix}1\\-1\end{pmatrix}.$
>>>
Therefore
$$\X=c_1\begin{pmatrix}3\\2\end{pmatrix}e^{4t}+c_2\begin{pmatrix}1\\-1\end{pmatrix}e^{-t},\qquad\text{i.e.}\qquad x=3c_1e^{4t}+c_2e^{-t},\quad y=2c_1e^{4t}-c_2e^{-t}.$$
>>>
The origin is a **saddle point**: trajectories come in along $\K_2$ and leave along $\K_1$.

```field x=[-4,4] y=[-4,4] caption="Phase portrait of $\X'=\begin{pmatrix}2&3\\2&1\end{pmatrix}\X$"
matrix [2 3; 2 1]
trajectories from (1,0.2) (-1,-0.2) (0.2,1) (-0.2,-1) (2,-2.3) (-2,2.3) (3,-2) (-3,2) (0.5,-0.6) (-0.5,0.6)
eigenlines on
classify on
```
:::

::: example {#ex:ivp}
Solve the initial-value problem
$$\X'(t)=\begin{pmatrix}1&2&-1\\1&0&1\\4&-4&5\end{pmatrix}\X(t),\qquad \X(0)=\begin{pmatrix}-1\\0\\0\end{pmatrix}.$$
:::

::: solution space=16
Expanding along the first row,
$$|\A-\lambda I|=(1-\lambda)(\lambda^2-5\lambda+4)-2(1-\lambda)+4(1-\lambda)=(1-\lambda)(\lambda^2-5\lambda+6)=-(\lambda-1)(\lambda-2)(\lambda-3).$$

$\lambda_1=1$: $\begin{pmatrix}0&2&-1\\1&-1&1\\4&-4&4\end{pmatrix}\K=\mathbf 0\Rightarrow k_3=2k_2,\ k_1=k_2-k_3=-k_2;\quad \K_1=(-1,1,2)^T.$

$\lambda_2=2$: $\begin{pmatrix}-1&2&-1\\1&-2&1\\4&-4&3\end{pmatrix}\K=\mathbf 0\Rightarrow k_1=2k_2-k_3,\ 4k_2-k_3=0;\quad \K_2=(-2,1,4)^T.$

$\lambda_3=3$: $\begin{pmatrix}-2&2&-1\\1&-3&1\\4&-4&2\end{pmatrix}\K=\mathbf 0\Rightarrow k_1=-k_2,\ k_3=4k_2;\quad \K_3=(-1,1,4)^T.$

General solution:
$$\X=c_1\begin{pmatrix}-1\\1\\2\end{pmatrix}e^{t}+c_2\begin{pmatrix}-2\\1\\4\end{pmatrix}e^{2t}+c_3\begin{pmatrix}-1\\1\\4\end{pmatrix}e^{3t}.$$
Initial condition $\X(0)=(-1,0,0)^T$:
$$-c_1-2c_2-c_3=-1,\qquad c_1+c_2+c_3=0,\qquad 2c_1+4c_2+4c_3=0.$$
From the last two equations $c_1=0$ and $c_3=-c_2$; then the first gives $c_2=1,\ c_3=-1$. Hence
$$\X(t)=\begin{pmatrix}-2\\1\\4\end{pmatrix}e^{2t}-\begin{pmatrix}-1\\1\\4\end{pmatrix}e^{3t}=\begin{pmatrix}-2e^{2t}+e^{3t}\\e^{2t}-e^{3t}\\4e^{2t}-4e^{3t}\end{pmatrix}.$$

```plot x=[0,1] y=[-12,2] caption="The three components of $\X(t)$"
f(x) = -2exp(2x) + exp(3x) color=blue label="$x_1$" at=0.75
g(x) = exp(2x) - exp(3x) color=red label="$x_2$" at=0.85
h(x) = 4exp(2x) - 4exp(3x) color=green label="$x_3$" at=0.55
```
:::

### Repeated eigenvalues

If $(\lambda-\lambda_1)^m$ is a factor of the characteristic polynomial but $(\lambda-\lambda_1)^{m+1}$ is not, $\lambda_1$ is an eigenvalue of [[multiplicity $m$]]. We discuss two situations:

- **Case I:** $\lambda_1$ has $m$ linearly independent eigenvectors (in particular, $\A_{n\times n}$ has $n$ independent eigenvectors).
- **Case II:** $\lambda_1$ has only one independent eigenvector (Lecture 5).

In Case I, with independent eigenvectors $\K_1,\dots,\K_m$ for $\lambda_1$, the general solution contains $c_1\K_1e^{\lambda_1t}+\cdots+c_m\K_me^{\lambda_1t}$, exactly as in the distinct case.

::: example {#ex:case1}
Find a general solution of $\X'=\A\X$, where $\A=\begin{pmatrix}2&0&0\\1&1&2\\1&-1&4\end{pmatrix}$.
:::

::: solution space=12
Expanding along the first row, $|\A-\lambda I|=(2-\lambda)\big[(1-\lambda)(4-\lambda)+2\big]=(2-\lambda)(\lambda^2-5\lambda+6)=-(\lambda-2)^2(\lambda-3)$. So $\lambda_1=\lambda_2=2$ (multiplicity 2) and $\lambda_3=3$.

$\lambda=2$: $\begin{pmatrix}0&0&0\\1&-1&2\\1&-1&2\end{pmatrix}\K=\mathbf 0\Rightarrow k_1=k_2-2k_3$, with $k_2,k_3$ free:
$$\K=k_2\begin{pmatrix}1\\1\\0\end{pmatrix}+k_3\begin{pmatrix}-2\\0\\1\end{pmatrix}\Rightarrow \K_1=\begin{pmatrix}1\\1\\0\end{pmatrix},\ \K_2=\begin{pmatrix}-2\\0\\1\end{pmatrix}\quad\text{(Case I).}$$

$\lambda=3$: $\begin{pmatrix}-1&0&0\\1&-2&2\\1&-1&1\end{pmatrix}\K=\mathbf 0\Rightarrow k_1=0,\ k_2=k_3;\quad \K_3=(0,1,1)^T.$

::: answer
$\X=c_1\begin{pmatrix}1\\1\\0\end{pmatrix}e^{2t}+c_2\begin{pmatrix}-2\\0\\1\end{pmatrix}e^{2t}+c_3\begin{pmatrix}0\\1\\1\end{pmatrix}e^{3t}.$
:::
:::

::: recall "Transpose and symmetric matrices" from="Matrix Theory"
- The transpose of $A=[a_{ij}]$ is $A^T=[a_{ji}]$; e.g. $A=\begin{pmatrix}2&13\\-9&11\\3&17\end{pmatrix}_{3\times2}\Rightarrow A^T=\begin{pmatrix}2&-9&3\\13&11&17\end{pmatrix}_{2\times3}.$
- A [[real symmetric matrix]] is a matrix with real entries satisfying $A=A^T$; e.g. $A=A^T=\begin{pmatrix}1&-2&2\\-2&1&2\\2&2&1\end{pmatrix}$.
:::

::: theorem "Eigenvectors of a real symmetric matrix" {#thm:sym}
If $\A$ is an $n\times n$ real symmetric matrix, then all its eigenvalues are real and $\A$ always has $n$ linearly independent eigenvectors. Consequently, a repeated eigenvalue of a symmetric matrix is always of Case I.
:::

::: example {#ex:sym}
Find a general solution of $\X'(t)=\A\X(t)$, where $\A=\begin{pmatrix}1&-2&2\\-2&1&2\\2&2&1\end{pmatrix}$.

*Hint:* $\lambda_1=\lambda_2=3,\ \lambda_3=-3$.
:::

::: solution space=12
(Indeed $|\A-\lambda I|=-(\lambda-3)^2(\lambda+3)$.)

$\lambda=3$: $\begin{pmatrix}-2&-2&2\\-2&-2&2\\2&2&-2\end{pmatrix}\K=\mathbf 0\Rightarrow k_1+k_2-k_3=0$:
$$\K=k_3\begin{pmatrix}1\\0\\1\end{pmatrix}+k_2\begin{pmatrix}-1\\1\\0\end{pmatrix}\Rightarrow\K_1=\begin{pmatrix}1\\0\\1\end{pmatrix},\ \K_2=\begin{pmatrix}-1\\1\\0\end{pmatrix}.$$

$\lambda=-3$: $\begin{pmatrix}4&-2&2\\-2&4&2\\2&2&4\end{pmatrix}\K=\mathbf 0\Rightarrow k_1=-k_3,\ k_2=-k_3;\quad\K_3=(-1,-1,1)^T.$

As predicted by @thm:sym (two eigenvectors for the double eigenvalue),
$$\X=c_1\begin{pmatrix}1\\0\\1\end{pmatrix}e^{3t}+c_2\begin{pmatrix}-1\\1\\0\end{pmatrix}e^{3t}+c_3\begin{pmatrix}-1\\-1\\1\end{pmatrix}e^{-3t}.$$
:::

::: teacher
Draw the saddle portrait (@ex:saddle) before writing the formula — students remember the geometry.
:::

::: mcq
Q: Substituting $\X=\K e^{\lambda t}$ into $\X'=\A\X$ leads to
- $(\A+\lambda I)\K=\mathbf 0$
- $(\A-\lambda I)\K=\mathbf 0$ *
- $\A\K=\mathbf 0$
- $\lambda\A=\K$
> $\lambda e^{\lambda t}\K=e^{\lambda t}\A\K$.

Q: The general solution of $\X'=\begin{pmatrix}1&0\\0&-3\end{pmatrix}\X$ is
- $c_1\begin{pmatrix}1\\0\end{pmatrix}e^{t}+c_2\begin{pmatrix}0\\1\end{pmatrix}e^{-3t}$ *
- $c_1\begin{pmatrix}1\\0\end{pmatrix}e^{-3t}+c_2\begin{pmatrix}0\\1\end{pmatrix}e^{t}$
- $c_1\begin{pmatrix}1\\1\end{pmatrix}e^{t}+c_2\begin{pmatrix}1\\-1\end{pmatrix}e^{-3t}$
- $c\begin{pmatrix}1\\-3\end{pmatrix}e^{t}$
> Diagonal matrix: eigenvalues $1,-3$ with eigenvectors $\mathbf e_1,\mathbf e_2$.

Q: The general solution of $\X'=\begin{pmatrix}1&2\\2&1\end{pmatrix}\X$ is
- $c_1\begin{pmatrix}1\\1\end{pmatrix}e^{-t}+c_2\begin{pmatrix}1\\-1\end{pmatrix}e^{3t}$
- $c_1\begin{pmatrix}1\\1\end{pmatrix}e^{3t}+c_2\begin{pmatrix}1\\-1\end{pmatrix}e^{-t}$ *
- $c_1\begin{pmatrix}1\\2\end{pmatrix}e^{t}+c_2\begin{pmatrix}2\\1\end{pmatrix}e^{t}$
- $c_1\begin{pmatrix}1\\0\end{pmatrix}e^{3t}+c_2\begin{pmatrix}0\\1\end{pmatrix}e^{-t}$
> Eigenvalues $3$ and $-1$, with eigenvectors $(1,1)^T$ and $(1,-1)^T$.

Q: A real symmetric $3\times3$ matrix $\A$ has eigenvalues $2,2,5$. The general solution of $\X'=\A\X$ has the form
- $c_1\K_1e^{2t}+c_2\K_2e^{2t}+c_3\K_3e^{5t}$ (no $te^{2t}$ terms) *
- $c_1\K_1e^{2t}+c_2(\K_1te^{2t}+\K_2e^{2t})+c_3\K_3e^{5t}$
- $c_1\K_1e^{2t}+c_2\K_3e^{5t}$
- it cannot be determined
> @thm:sym: a symmetric matrix always has a full set of eigenvectors (Case I).

Q: The solution of $\X'=\begin{pmatrix}2&0\\0&3\end{pmatrix}\X,\ \X(0)=\begin{pmatrix}1\\-1\end{pmatrix}$, is
- $\begin{pmatrix}e^{3t}\\-e^{2t}\end{pmatrix}$
- $\begin{pmatrix}e^{2t}\\-e^{3t}\end{pmatrix}$ *
- $\begin{pmatrix}e^{2t}\\e^{3t}\end{pmatrix}$
- $\begin{pmatrix}-e^{2t}\\e^{3t}\end{pmatrix}$
> $\X=c_1\mathbf e_1e^{2t}+c_2\mathbf e_2e^{3t}$ with $c_1=1,\ c_2=-1$.
:::
